{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When a man makes a singular vow, etc.</b> This pericope is juxtaposed to the curses to say that Israel make vows in a time of trouble — like Jacob, who would vow in a time of trouble to give a tithe of all. But not like Jacob — of all, absolutely all — for Jacob, even after the trouble had passed, did not depart from the good path; but for the generations, this is their way — a slippery slope: they vow in a time of trouble, and once the storm of trouble and distress has passed, they regret it and return to their former state. This is like the reed with which Ahijah the Shilonite cursed Israel (I Kings 14:15), for this reed is subdued only when the wind blows through it, and when the wind subsides it stands back up, erect as at first. So Israel are subdued only when trouble presses them; and once Israel see that relief has come, they grow stout of heart, for once the compelling force departs they revert to their former state. And that which Ba'al ha-Turim explained (27:1) supports our words — that the total number of the shekels is 143, corresponding to the 143 curses in the Priestly Torah and in Mishneh Torah. But is it merely because their number is equal that they were juxtaposed to one another? Rather, it is to tell you that the 143 curses of the reproof give rise to 143 shekels' worth of vows, for Israel do not vow to give of their money for the sake of HaShem except in a time when troubles compel them — as will be explained, with the help of HaShem, below in the pericope of וַיֵּלֶךְ (Deuteronomy 31:17), on the verse ״וְהָיָה כִּי תִמְצֶאןָ אוֹתוֹ רָעוֹת רַבּוֹת וְצָרוֹת״, which the Sages of blessed memory expounded (Ḥagigah 5a) concerning one who lends to the poor in the time of his distress. But this exposition is far-fetched to accept, for all the troubles do not compel them to confession except this particular trouble. Therefore it is my desire to explain that this speaks of one who lends to the poor specifically when trouble comes — that is, in the time of the lender's distress, for troubles press him to this: to vow vows for charity. And the error of one who vows in this manner will be explained, with the help of HaShem, below in the pericope of וַיֵּלֶךְ. Therefore the pericope of כִּי יַפְלִיא was juxtaposed to the pericope of the reproof, and Scripture speaks in the present tense. But whatever a person does without compulsion, from willingness — this is called ״יוֹם רָצוֹן לַה׳״ (Isaiah 58:5), and it produces favor from HaShem (Proverbs 8:35). <br> <b>The book of Leviticus is complete — with the help of the great, mighty, and awesome El.</b>"], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:39:03.820209696Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}